<html>
<title>Switch Mnemonic</title>
<body>


<h1>Switch Mnemonic</h1>


<h2>Op codes</h2>

<p>
There are a few 1802 op codes with multiple mnemonics:
</p>
<ul>
<li><tt>33</tt>: <strong>BDF</strong>, BPZ or BGE</li>
<li><tt>38</tt>: <strong>NBR</strong> or SKP</li>
<li><tt>3B</tt>: <strong>BNF</strong>, BM or BL</li>
<li><tt>76</tt>: <strong>SHRC</strong> or RSHR</li>
<li><tt>7E</tt>: <strong>SHLC</strong> or RSHL</li>
<li><tt>C8</tt>: <strong>NLBR</strong> or LSKP</li>
</ul>

<p>
All variants are accepted in the assembler. The variant used will be stored as a type. This can also be changed via the procedure described in the <a href="DirectMarking.htm">Memory Type Selection</a> section, using the <strong>Switch</strong> marking type. When no type is specified, the mnemonics shown in bold will be used.
</p>


<h2>Example</h2>

<p>
For example, if the type is set to <strong>Switch</strong> (1) and the <strong>Mark</strong> button (2) is pressed on address 0000 (hexadecimal) in the following example:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_switch_mark.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Switch</strong> type selection</li>
<li>(2) <strong>Mark</strong> button</li>
</ul>

<p>
the code will change to:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_switch_mark_result.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>

<p>
Pressing <strong>Mark</strong> again on address 0000 (hexadecimal) and the code will change to:
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/assembler_switch_mark_result2.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
</body>
</html>
